//@ normative
//@ ok
@define printf(fmt: string, ...) <noret>;

// Line 5 used to read "Truncated (Rust-Like upcoming...)" and both halves of it are gone,
// 2026-09-07. The Rust-like arms form is not upcoming: ADR 0023 refused it and step 1 deleted
// the `macro name { ... }` production, so that spelling is a syntax error rather than a
// pending feature. And the file is no longer truncated -- the macro importing.fin:23 calls
// is written below, which is ADR 0023 step 8.
//
// `magic_add` is the form ADR 0023 settled: named parameters, one `quote` returning one
// expression, and `$a`/`$b` unquotes. The body spells nothing but its own parameters and an
// operator, which is all a body may spell (ADR 0020) -- so it needs no qualifier and no
// import to be correct anywhere it is expanded. importing.fin:7 imports this file as a whole
// module and calls it `macros.magic_add!(10, 20)`; the parenthesised bracket keeps each
// argument an expression, so `10` and `20` arrive as themselves and the expansion is `10 + 20`.
@macro magic_add(a, b) { return quote { $a + $b; }; }
